# FDF Angular Development Standards

## Mandatory: Use the Angular Developer Skill

Before creating or substantially editing any Angular artifact, read and follow:
`.agents/skills/angular-developer/SKILL.md`

This applies to:
- Creating or editing components
- Creating or editing services
- Routing and route guards
- Reactive and template-driven forms
- Angular Signals, linkedSignal, resource
- Dependency injection
- Reusable UI abstractions
- Accessibility (ARIA) in Angular
- Angular testing (unit + integration)
- Architecture refactoring

## Project Context

- **Framework**: Angular 22 (standalone components, Signals-first)
- **UI Library**: Angular Material 22
- **Package manager**: npm
- **Backend**: Node.js/Express REST API
- **Auth**: Google Sign-In (OAuth/OIDC) for Directors; JWT for backend validation
- **Roles**: `Director` — group ownership enforced server-side

## Architecture Rules

- Use **standalone components** — do not introduce NgModules unless already present.
- Use **Signals** for local reactive state (avoid `BehaviorSubject` for new code).
- Use `inject()` instead of constructor injection for new services.
- Follow **lazy-loaded routes** as already established in `app.routes.ts`.
- Do not bypass the existing `AuthGuard`/`RoleGuard` pattern.
- Server-side authorization must never be replaced by frontend-only guard logic.

## Code Quality

- Every public service method needs a JSDoc comment for non-trivial logic.
- Never use `any` without justification — prefer typed models from `src/app/models/`.
- Keep components focused — extract logic into services if business logic creeps in.
- Run `ng lint` and `ng build` before declaring work complete.

## UI Completion Gate

Before declaring a UI change complete, verify:
- [ ] Correct Angular patterns used (Signals, standalone, inject())
- [ ] UI/UX design review performed (`frontend-design-review` skill)
- [ ] Desktop layout verified
- [ ] Mobile layout verified (no horizontal scroll, touch targets ≥ 44px)
- [ ] Keyboard operation verified
- [ ] Accessibility checked (roles, labels, live regions)
- [ ] Loading state present where async operations occur
- [ ] Empty state handled
- [ ] Error state handled with user-readable messages
- [ ] Validation errors accessible and descriptive
- [ ] Role permissions respected (backend authorization also in place)
- [ ] Playwright E2E test created or updated
- [ ] Relevant Playwright test passes
- [ ] Security implications reviewed (see `fdf-security.mdc`)
